From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] dhcp6c: take a stateless reply without a Server Identifier

RFC 8415 (as RFC 3315 before it) has a client discard a Reply without a
Server Identifier option, and dhcp6c does. QEMU's user network, libslirp
4.x (dhcpv6.c), answers Information-Request with the DNS servers and the
client's ID but no server ID, so dhcp6c logged "no server ID option" and
retried forever. A reply to Information-Request configures nothing a
server ID would name later (no lease, no renewal to that server), so it
is now taken with a notice; replies in every other state still need one.

Rebase-risk: low (one check in client6_recvreply()).
--- a/dhcp6c.c
+++ b/dhcp6c.c
@@ -1787,10 +1787,21 @@
 		break;          
 	}
 
-	/* A Reply message must contain a Server ID option */
+	/*
+	 * A Reply message must contain a Server ID option. QEMU's user
+	 * network (libslirp's dhcpv6.c), a common place to meet DHCPv6,
+	 * answers an Information-Request without one; a stateless reply
+	 * configures no lease that the server's ID would identify, so it
+	 * is taken, with a note.
+	 */
 	if (optinfo->serverID.duid_len == 0) {
-		d_printf(LOG_INFO, FNAME, "no server ID option");
-		return (-1);
+		if (state != DHCP6S_INFOREQ) {
+			d_printf(LOG_INFO, FNAME, "no server ID option");
+			return (-1);
+		}
+		d_printf(LOG_NOTICE, FNAME,
+		    "no server ID option in a reply to Information-Request; "
+		    "taken");
 	}
 
 	/*
